of gonococcal urethritis and unprotected sexual activity"], "excluded_candidates": ["Subacute Progressive Hepatic Dysfunction: Lower-tier due to acute presentation and hemodynamic instability", "Alcohol-Induced Acute Pancreatitis: Possible but less likely due to specific clinical features of catastrophic intra-abdominal process", "Exercise-Induced Ruptured Ovarian Cyst: Considered but requires imaging confirmation"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension and tachycardia (shock state)", "Acute right upper quadrant pain with diffuse tenderness", "High-risk sexual behavior with possible ongoing reproductive complications", "Recent rapid weight loss (potential nutritional/metabolic decompensation)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic / Pelvic Catastrophe", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.35, "posterior": 0.47368421052631576, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Heavy alcohol use moderately supports acute pancreatic catastrophe (B4), but absence of back pain and elevated amylase/lipase weakens this link."], "evidence_against": ["The gradual onset with acute exacerbation during exercise moderately argues against gynecologic/pelvic catastrophe (B1) but remains compatible with chronic-to-…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic / Hepatobiliary Vascular Catastrophe", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.3383458646616541, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol use and rapid weight loss suggest chronic liver damage (B2), but the acute presentation with shock argues against purely chronic processes."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Obstruction with Hemodynamic Impact", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.113, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of obstructive jaundice signs weakly disfavors biliary obstruction with hemodynamic impact (B3) but doesn't exclude acute hepatic insults."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Pancreatic Catastrophe", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.075, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Missing back pain and normal pancreatic labs strongly argue against acute pancreatic catastrophe (B4), favoring other causes of RUQ pain and shock."]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the patient's right upper quadrant pain, hypotension, and recent rapid weight loss indicate biliary obstruction with secondary hemodynamic compromise (e.g., obstructive jaundice with sepsis)?", "summary": "Weight loss alone doesn't strongly support biliary obstruction (B3), but combined with hypotension, it raises suspicion of a secondary hemodynamic impact from obstruction."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's daily alcohol consumption directly support alcoholic acute pancreatitis as a catastrophic cause of hypotension and RUQ pain?", "summary": "Heavy alcohol use moderately supports acute pancreatic catastrophe (B4), but absence of back pain and elevated amylase/lipase weakens this link."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute progression of pain over days before sudden exacerbation during exercise argue against a gyneco